			"25": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": true
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "COLLECTIONS\n\nAlright kid, by now you should be pretty familiar with how to create collections.\nIn the previous collection tutorials you have learned how to create collections from pattern nodes, how to manually instantiate nodes and how to expand existing collections.\n\nNow it is time to dig one level deeper and look at how we can manipulate collections.\nOne way to look at collections is as a list of values and there is a lot you can do with a list.\nPick a single value, swap values around, replace a value, add a value and so on.\nThen there is information about the collection, like the size and indices within the collection.\n\nIn this tutorial we'll go through all these nodes to get you more familiar with collection operations.­on't worry, you don't need to memorize all these nodes.\nThe goal of this tutorial is to introduce you to some of the concepts these nodes have in common."
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 288.0228271484375,
					"width": 866.5262451171875,
					"x": -304.398223876953125,
					"y": -90.57525634765625
				},
				"class": {
					"id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
					"version": 1
				},
				"clock": "video",
				"color": "ffd0c117",
				"constants": {},
				"hidden": [
					"text",
					"alignment",
					"font-size",
					"fill"
				],
				"name": "Comment",
				"thumbnail_visible": true
			},
			"26": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "READING & WRITING\n\nThe Read and Write nodes can be used to read values from a collection or overwrite them.\n\nThe Read node takes a collection and an index and returns the value at the given index.\nThe Write node overwrites a value in the collection at a given index.\nThe Span node works similar to the Read node, but is used to read a subsection of the collection.\n\nIn this tutorial we'll be frequently manipulating Linear nodes in order to visualize what we're doing.\n\nLooking at the example we see how the Read node reads to color at index 5 from the collection.\nWhen looking at the Write node we can clearly see how the Write node overwrites index 5 with orange.\n"
					}
				},
